    {
      "id": "claim_6086977ee6544801977c322ee632616c",
      "statement": "The 2026-03 long-credit overweight is a demand-and-supply flow view: it expects corporate defined-benefit plans to buy long corporate paper through 2028, with demand large relative to net long-end issuance; net long-dated corporate issuance had been below that demand for six quarters.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal_research/FI/US/PENSION-LDI/2026-03.md",
          "version": "repo-file-v1:sha256:f3fd6fad91d3b8346cfbe9f6a408d6f749a82e942e73996e04caddbef8e1b6bb"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_abf4f327a3ad4657826d2a9e77e5fd86",
      "statement": "The FI-US-PENSION-LDI expression is bounded to the long end and single-A-and-better credit because it is a flow call rather than compensation for lower credit quality.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal_research/FI/US/PENSION-LDI/2026-03.md",
          "version": "repo-file-v1:sha256:f3fd6fad91d3b8346cfbe9f6a408d6f749a82e942e73996e04caddbef8e1b6bb"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8a90f0c0e24b413baec576330db7b3bd",
      "statement": "The load-bearing premise of FI-US-PENSION-LDI 2026-03 is that pension funding rules make surplus worth protecting through contribution requirements and liability discount-rate measurement; allowing surplus release or re-risking could reverse flows and invalidate the overweight.",
      "evidence": [
